The paper catalog writes the locality once at the top of the first sheet and
leaves it blank on every sheet that follows. Anyone reading a later sheet has to
assume it belongs to the last locality written—an assumption that is wrong for
every specimen caught at Pasir Putih.

{% aside type="note" title="Standard museum best practice" %}
Writing the full locality on every page of a paper catalog—even when it repeats
the previous page—is standard museum best practice. Each page then stands on its
own if it is copied, scanned out of order, or separated from the notebook, and a
change of locality is impossible to miss. Young Heru did not follow that
practice: only the first sheet carries a locality, and the Pasir Putih sheets
are blank.
{% /aside %}

Below is the top of the sheet holding HH 520–523. HH 520 came from Line 3 at
Pasir Putih, across the lake from Pondok Merah, yet the `Locality` header is
empty:

![Paper specimen sheet with an empty Locality header above the record for HH 520, Hylomys suillus, from trap line 3](../../../../../assets/images/tutorials/basic/paper-blank-locality.jpg)

In NAHPU, a **site** is a place you define once and reuse—a camp, a trap line,
or a pitfall line. Specimens never store their own locality text; they link to
an event, and the event links to its site. Every specimen inherits the right
place without anyone rewriting it.

## 1. Plan the sites

The journal describes five lines. Because Line 1 combined pitfalls and snap
traps, split it into two sites—one per method. Add the camp at Pondok Merah,
where the team slept, prepared specimens, and wrote the journal, for seven sites
in total.

The Site IDs follow the [NAHPU convention](/en/usages/sites/):

- a two-letter locality code (`TJ` for Tujuh),
- `CAMP` and a number for a camp, or the line leader's initials plus `L` (trap
  line) or `P` (pitfall line), and
- the original line number from the paper catalog.

| Site ID | Paper name | Area | What was set | Leader |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `TJ-CAMP1` | Not named | Pondok Merah | Camp beside the lake | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-L1` | Line 1 | Pondok Merah | 31 snap traps | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-P1` | Line 1 | Pondok Merah | 10 pitfalls, about 50 m from camp | Heru Handika |
| `TJBS-L2` | Line 2 | Pondok Merah | 25 snap traps, most 1–2 m above ground | Bima Sampel |
| `TJHH-L3` | Line 3 | Pasir Putih | 20 snap traps | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-P4` | Line 4 | Pasir Putih | Pitfalls (count not recorded) | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-L5` | Line 5 | Pasir Putih | 20 snap traps, many on lianas | Heru Handika |

Keeping the paper's line numbers in the Site IDs makes it easy to cross-check
any NAHPU record against the original forms.

## 2. Create the first site

{% steps %}

1. Open `Sites` and select `+`.
2. Enter `TJHH-L1` as `Site ID`, select `Heru Handika` as `Site Leader`, and
   choose a trap-line `Site Type`.
3. Enter the geography from the paper header:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Country` | `Indonesia` |
    | `State/Province` | `Jambi` |
    | `County/Parish/District` | `Kerinci` |
    | `Precise Locality` | `Gunung Tujuh, Sungai Jernih, Pondok Merah, beside Lake Gunung Tujuh` |

    The paper writes `Mt. Tujuh`. Using the full local name, *Gunung Tujuh*,
    keeps the locality aligned with gazetteers. Leave fields you cannot verify—
    such as `Municipality/City/Town`—empty.

4. Enter the habitat. On paper, habitat appears only on HH 436
   (`Mature secondary forest`). The 12 September journal entry adds that the
   forest is similar along all Pondok Merah lines and slightly disturbed by
   hikers.

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | Type | `Montane forest` |
    | Condition | `Slightly disturbed` |
    | Description | `Mature secondary forest, similar along all Pondok Merah lines. Slightly disturbed by hikers.` |

    `Type` and `Condition` come from lists in `Settings`; add any missing value
    there.

    Habitat describes a place, not an individual specimen. Recorded on the site,
    it automatically applies to every specimen from the line rather than just
    the one form where it happened to be written.

{% /steps %}

## 3. Add the coordinate

The paper header gives one GPS point for all of Pondok Merah:

```text
01.70274° S   101.39032° E   Elev. 2004 m
```

Before entering it, address three georeferencing issues:

- **Sign.** Decimal degrees express hemisphere with a sign rather than a letter.
  South is negative, so latitude is `-1.70274`. Dropping the `S` would place the
  site in the northern hemisphere, roughly 380 km away.
- **Unit.** Sheet 1 crosses out `feet` for `m`, and sheet 2 writes `m asl`.
  NAHPU always stores elevation in meters, so enter `2004` with no unit
  ambiguity.
- **Datum and missing uncertainty.** The paper records neither a datum nor a GPS
  uncertainty. Enter `WGS84` as the datum here—not only because it is the most
  common GPS datum, but also because Heru has consistently used WGS84, and it is
  the older Heru writing this tutorial. Imagine if someone else were digitizing
  these sheets years later without that insider knowledge: they would have to
  leave the datum unknown or risk guessing wrong. That is where NAHPU shines—by
  prompting for datum and uncertainty up front (and flagging blank values with
  `?`), NAHPU catches critical missing georeference information while you are
  still in the field. Leave `Uncertainty` empty; never invent an accuracy value
  you do not have.

{% steps %}

1. On the site page, open the `Coordinates` panel and select `Add coordinate`.
2. Choose decimal degrees (`DD`) and enter the values:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | Name | `TJHH-PM` |
    | Decimal latitude | `-1.70274` |
    | Decimal longitude | `101.39032` |
    | Elevation | `2004` |
    | Datum | `WGS84` |
    | Uncertainty | Leave empty |
    | Notes | `One point recorded for all Pondok Merah lines in the 2014 paper catalog. Datum set to WGS84 based on Heru's consistent GPS usage; uncertainty not recorded.` |

3. Select `Add`, then verify the pin on the map—it should sit beside Lake Gunung
   Tujuh.

{% /steps %}

Below is the finished site. In this screenshot, both datum and uncertainty show
`?` because both were left empty; after entering `WGS84` above, your record will
display `WGS84` for the datum and `?` only for the missing uncertainty:

![NAHPU Sites page on an iPad for TJHH-L1, with Indonesia, Jambi, Kerinci, the Pondok Merah locality, montane forest habitat, and coordinate TJHH-PM at -1.70274, 101.39032, 2004 m](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/site-tjhh-l1.webp)

## 4. Create the other Pondok Merah sites

{% steps %}

1. With `TJHH-L1` open, select `Duplicate site` from the three-dot menu.
2. Enter `TJHH-P1` as `Site ID` and change `Site Type` to a pitfall line.
3. Add the `TJHH-PM` coordinate again (duplicating a site does not copy its
   coordinates).
4. Repeat for `TJBS-L2`, setting `Bima Sampel` as `Site Leader` and adding
   `Most snap traps set 1–2 m above the ground.` to `Remarks`.
5. Repeat for the camp, `TJ-CAMP1`, using a camp `Site Type` and the same
   habitat. You will link the field journal entries to this site in
   [Basic tutorial 6](/en/tutorials/basic/narrative/).

{% /steps %}

## 5. Create the Pasir Putih sites

Pasir Putih lies across the lake, and the journal describes each of its three
lines differently. Create them from `+` rather than duplicating so no Pondok
Merah value carries over by mistake.

Use the same country (`Indonesia`), province (`Jambi`), and county (`Kerinci`),
with `Montane forest` as the habitat type for all three:

| Site ID | `Precise Locality` | Condition | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `TJHH-L3` | `Gunung Tujuh, Pasir Putih, across Lake Gunung Tujuh from Pondok Merah` | `Undisturbed` | `Many moss-covered trees 1–2 m in diameter. Open ground with few small plants. One rarely used trail.` |
| `TJHH-P4` | Same as `TJHH-L3` | `Undisturbed` | `Open canopy, apparently from natural processes. Dense ground cover; fewer large trees than Line 3.` |
| `TJHH-L5` | Same as `TJHH-L3` | `Undisturbed` | `Open in places and closed in others. Many lianas. Moss cover similar to Line 3.` |

The paper catalog has **no coordinates** for Pasir Putih. Leave the coordinate
panel empty and add this note to each site's `Remarks`:

```text
No coordinates recorded in the 2014 paper catalog. About 1.5 hours by boat across the lake from Pondok Merah.
```

{% aside type="caution" title="Do not reuse the camp coordinate" %}
It is tempting to copy the Pondok Merah point so the Pasir Putih records "have
coordinates", but that would place them on the wrong side of the lake. An empty
coordinate is honest and easy to flag later; a wrong coordinate looks like valid
data. If you later georeference Pasir Putih from a map, document how you derived
the point and include a realistic uncertainty.
{% /aside %}

## What you gained over paper

- Every specimen inherits its locality from its site, including records from
  sheets with blank headers.
- Pondok Merah and Pasir Putih are distinct sites, so their specimens cannot be
  mixed up.
- Elevation uses one unit, latitude carries its sign, datum is recorded
  explicitly (`WGS84`), and missing georeference details—such as GPS uncertainty
  and the Pasir Putih coordinates—stay visible as empty fields rather than
  hidden gaps.
- Habitat is described once per site from the journal and the one specimen form
  that recorded it.

